# brief.toml

@lede
The project-level configuration file. Declares custom shortcodes, sets
strictness flags, and points at templates.
@end

A `brief.toml` at the project root tells the compiler this directory is a
Brief project. Cross-document references (`@ref`) resolve relative to the
nearest `brief.toml`. Without one, every `@ref` is @ec[B0604].

## A worked example

```toml
[project]
name = "my-docs"
version = "0.1.0"

[compile]
strict_heading_levels = true
default_target = "html"

[compile.llm]
minify_code_blocks = true
minify_languages = ["json", "jsonl"]
preserve_code_fences = true

[shortcodes.note]
kind = "block"
template_html = "<aside class=\"note\">{{content}}</aside>"
template_llm  = "[note] {{content}} [/note]"

[shortcodes.callout]
kind = "block"
arguments = { kind = { type = "string", required = true,
  oneof = ["note", "tip", "important", "warning", "caution"] } }
template_html = "<aside class=\"callout-{{args.kind}}\">{{content}}</aside>"
```

Unknown keys anywhere in `brief.toml` are a load error, not a silent
no-op — the same strictness the language applies to documents.

## Sections

### `[project]`

@dl
name
: Required. Free-form string. Used in error messages and the project index.
version
: Optional. SemVer string. Reserved for conformance reports; the
  compiler does not consume it today.
@end

### `[compile]`

@dl
default_target
: One of `html`, `llm`, `json`. Used when `brief compile` is run without
  `--target`.
strict_heading_levels
: Boolean. When true, skipping heading levels (e.g., `##` → `####`) is
  @ec[B0503]. Default false.
@end

### `[compile.llm]`

LLM-mode-specific knobs:

@dl
minify_code_blocks
: Boolean, default true. Master switch for code-block minification.
minify_languages
: Array of language tags. Default is the languages the compiler has a
  built-in minifier for. Forcing `python` or `yaml` here does nothing —
  they are hardcoded refused (@ec[B0704]).
preserve_code_fences
: Boolean, default true. When false, fences are dropped from minified
  blocks in LLM output. Saves a few tokens; loses structural cues.
@end

### `[shortcodes.<name>]`

Each entry registers one shortcode under that name. Fields:

@dl
kind
: Required. One of `inline`, `block`, or `both`.
arguments
: Optional. A table of argument specs. Each spec has `type` (one of
  `string`, `int`, `ident`, `array`), an optional `required` boolean,
  an optional `position` integer (for positional arguments), and an
  optional `oneof` array (for enum-constrained string values). Passing
  an argument the shortcode does not declare is @ec[B0409].
template_html
: An inline template string, mustache-style with `{{content}}` and
  `{{args.<name>}}` placeholders. Template files are not supported —
  the value is always the template itself.
template_llm
: Same as `template_html`, used when the compile target is `llm`.
@end

## Templates

Templates are *minimal* mustache. Placeholders only:

- `{{content}}` — the rendered children of the shortcode.
- `{{args.<name>}}` — the value of an argument by name.

There are no conditionals, no loops, no embedded code. Logic that wants
those belongs in a custom shortcode that wraps the conditional content,
not in the template itself.

## Where the compiler looks

The compiler walks up from the source file's directory until it finds a
`brief.toml`. That file's directory is the project root; cross-document
`@ref` paths are rooted there. If no `brief.toml` is found, the compiler
runs in standalone mode — every feature works *except* `@ref`.

## What's separate: `book.toml`

`brief-web` reads its own configuration file (`book.toml`) for site
title, theme, server port, and site-scoped shortcodes. The two files
coexist — `brief.toml` is the language layer, `book.toml` is the site
layer. Shortcodes registered in `book.toml` extend (do not replace) those
registered in `brief.toml`.
